1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to another lesson here at EnglishPod.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:07,410
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,230
And my name is Catherine.

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:12,510
And today, Marco, you and I are talking about movies, right?

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:17,010
We're talking a little bit about movies, but mostly we're having a big argument.

6
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:18,770
Okay, I kind of like arguments.

7
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:21,910
Lots of great words and phrases for you to learn here in English.

8
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:27,570
But we've got an upper intermediate level lesson with some people who are not getting along very well.

9
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,810
It's a couple, a guy and a girl, and they're having a fight, as couples do.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:36,900
So we're going to be learning a lot of the different language that they are using in this very heated debate.

11
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:41,360
So let's listen to this dialogue for the first time, and then we'll be right back.

12
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:51,920
Wow, that terrible movie is finally over.

13
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:54,930
Next time I'm picking the film because I don't want to end up seeing a chick flick.

14
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:56,780
Well, you should have picked.

15
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:58,780
In the end, you always complain about everything.

16
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:00,020
Not everything.

17
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:01,380
Just this film.

18
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:03,220
Even the title was ridiculous.

19
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,200
And it's so long.

20
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,780
Those were the two and a half most wasted hours of my life.

21
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:12,770
So much so that I'm thinking about asking them to give me my money back.

22
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:15,290
I'm thinking of taking you back home.

23
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:18,530
I thought we could have a nice evening, but you're always so negative.

24
00:01:19,170 --> 00:01:24,650
I'm only complaining about a movie that I could have rented or bought and then thrown in the garbage.

25
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:27,050
You see, that's what I'm talking about.

26
00:01:27,390 --> 00:01:29,690
I can't stand your sarcastic jokes anymore.

27
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:33,230
Next time, go with your gay friend who is more in touch with his feelings.

28
00:01:33,810 --> 00:01:35,290
Well, he's more of a man than you are.

29
00:01:35,790 --> 00:01:37,330
At least he appreciates love stories.

30
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:38,630
Love stories?

31
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:40,710
More like one night stands.

32
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:45,330
Don't criticize Mario, or else I'll start on those fat, drunk friends of yours.

33
00:01:45,730 --> 00:01:46,270
They're no saints.

34
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:47,250
My friends?

35
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:48,920
Fat?

36
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:50,460
What about those whales you call friends?

37
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:51,420
You're unbearable.

38
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:52,960
You can walk home.

39
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:53,380
I'm leaving.

40
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:07,560
I think the guy was exaggerating a little bit.

41
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:07,980
What do you think?

42
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:09,720
I think they were both exaggerating.

43
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:15,320
You know, by the end of an argument, both people are saying things that maybe they don't mean.

44
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:20,980
I think we'll talk a little bit more about this towards the end of the show, but now, why don't we take a look at language takeaway.

45
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:23,730
Language takeaway.

46
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:32,010
Alright on language takeaway today, we have four words and the first one that you probably saw in the very first sentence is a chick flick.

47
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:33,300
Chick flick.

48
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:36,560
So, first of all, the word chick means a woman.

49
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:38,160
It's slang and it's not very nice.

50
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:39,060
It's not very nice.

51
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:40,520
No.

52
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:46,780
Well, I mean, between guys, you would use it as like, oh, this chick or that chick, but you wouldn't really walk up to a girl and call her a chick, right?

53
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:48,140
No, you wouldn't say, hey, chick, what's up?

54
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,120
Not something you want to say.

55
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:57,280
So chick is a slang word for a girl or a woman and a chick flick is a girl's movie.

56
00:02:57,280 --> 00:02:58,380
So a flick is a movie.

57
00:02:59,080 --> 00:02:59,640
Another slang word.

58
00:03:00,230 --> 00:03:00,630
Exactly.

59
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:02,750
So this is the slang phrase for flick.

60
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:05,770
So hey, Marco, what flicks are showing at the movie theater?

61
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:07,120
All right.

62
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:08,120
Yeah.

63
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,120
So movies.

64
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:14,200
So in the chick flick is usually a romantic movie, a drama, something that's very, um.

65
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:15,200
Like beaches.

66
00:03:15,850 --> 00:03:16,930
Beaches is a chick flick.

67
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:18,090
I don't know if you've ever seen that.

68
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,400
I've never seen it.

69
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:22,880
So he's just not that into you is one of those new chick flicks.

70
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,280
Or like how to lose a guy in 10 days.

71
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:26,280
Yeah.

72
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:28,630
How to lose a guy in 10 days or the devil wears Prada.

73
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:30,120
Bride Wars.

74
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:31,120
Bride Wars.

75
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:32,120
Wow.

76
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:33,120
Lots of chick flicks.

77
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:35,320
I've actually never seen any one of these except how to lose a guy in 10 days.

78
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:36,880
I've actually only seen that one.

79
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:37,270
All right.

80
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:41,480
So chick flick is a girl's or woman's movie.

81
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,090
And next up we have a word, sarcastic.

82
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:45,410
We're talking about sarcastic jokes.

83
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:48,670
So sarcastic jokes or somebody being sarcastic.

84
00:03:49,310 --> 00:03:50,670
God, Marco, you're always so sarcastic.

85
00:03:51,130 --> 00:03:52,650
So what would sarcasm sound like?

86
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:56,920
Um, yeah, he's really funny.

87
00:03:56,920 --> 00:03:58,440
All right.

88
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:00,130
So it's not really what you're saying.

89
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:01,830
It's kind of how you're saying it.

90
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:02,680
Right.

91
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:05,940
So it's kind of like by saying, he's really funny.

92
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:07,560
You're saying, yeah, he's a funny guy.

93
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:10,520
But if I say, oh, he's really funny.

94
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,260
I'm saying he's not funny.

95
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:14,420
He's being mean or he's a jerk.

96
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:21,580
Sarcasm is kind of one of those things that it's a little bit difficult to learn how to do or to actually get it right.

97
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:23,140
Especially in English.

98
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:24,760
Right?

99
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:25,760
Yeah.

100
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:26,760
Marco, great explanation.

101
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:27,180
Exactly.

102
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:28,240
That's the way it goes.

103
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:28,600
Oh, yeah.

104
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:29,280
You're so funny.

105
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:31,760
So you're amazing.

106
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:32,760
You should go to you.

107
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:33,640
You went to sarcasm school.

108
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:34,760
So my parents like to say.

109
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:35,760
You can be really mean, Catherine.

110
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:36,760
Sorry.

111
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:37,760
Just kidding.

112
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:38,760
All right.

113
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:42,860
So apart from being sarcastic, you can criticize someone.

114
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:44,300
Like you're ever critical.

115
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:45,880
All right.

116
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:47,300
Sorry, I'll stop.

117
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:49,400
You're really into character right now.

118
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:49,620
All right.

119
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:57,440
So critical to criticize someone is to complain about them or to say that the things that they do are not very good.

120
00:04:57,720 --> 00:04:59,600
So you can criticize a person or a movie.

121
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:00,490
Yeah.

122
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:02,250
So this movie wasn't very good.

123
00:05:02,430 --> 00:05:03,050
It wasn't funny.

124
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:04,270
The actors were bad.

125
00:05:04,510 --> 00:05:05,590
It was really long.

126
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:06,650
These are criticisms.

127
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:07,790
Criticisms.

128
00:05:07,790 --> 00:05:08,350
All right.

129
00:05:08,830 --> 00:05:12,710
Well, and then moving on, the girl was finally tired of the guy and his complaints.

130
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:15,110
And she said he's unbearable.

131
00:05:15,990 --> 00:05:17,030
Gosh, you're unbearable.

132
00:05:17,630 --> 00:05:22,350
So the root of this word is to be able to like to stand something, to bear it.

133
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:27,370
And so if you're unbearable, something is unbearable, you just can't stand it.

134
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:27,940
All right.

135
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:29,140
So you're tired of it.

136
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:30,160
You can't stand it.

137
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:33,840
She thinks he's so unbearable, she makes him get out of the car.

138
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:34,560
And she leaves.

139
00:05:35,240 --> 00:05:36,240
Yeah.

140
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:37,240
She said you can walk home.

141
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:37,400
All right.

142
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:39,820
So that's an unbearable person, right?

143
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:41,480
Okay.

144
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:45,400
So we took a look at those four main words here in our dialogue.

145
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:49,900
Let's move on now to some of the phrases that we found in Fluency Builder.

146
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:52,220
Fluency Builder.

147
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:59,960
This first one's a common one, especially when we talk about women and men and the relationship between women and men.

148
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:06,280
We say about men that maybe they should be more in touch with their feelings or their feminine side.

149
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:07,280
Yeah.

150
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:13,260
Okay, so if you're saying to be more in touch with your feminine side, what exactly are you saying?

151
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,000
It's a good question.

152
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:25,030
Well, what you're saying is that you want to be able to express your feelings more or you want to be able to understand more about your feminine side or something like this.

153
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:28,460
So to be in touch with is more about yourself.

154
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:32,120
It's about self-reflection and self-understanding.

155
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:36,280
So you could say, I'm a very serious person, but I want to make some changes in my life.

156
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:39,320
So I'm trying to be more in touch with my inner child.

157
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:40,100
Your inner child.

158
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:41,320
All right.

159
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:47,100
So to be in touch with, and it's usually related to feelings, to something intangible.

160
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:47,960
Or to behavior.

161
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:49,480
To behavior.

162
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:50,140
All right.

163
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:54,680
Moving on now, a heated phrase, a one night stand.

164
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:57,000
Oh, I'll let you explain this one, Marco.

165
00:06:57,640 --> 00:06:57,860
All right.

166
00:06:57,860 --> 00:07:05,620
So a one night stand is a casual and very quick temporal encounter that's usually a sexual encounter.

167
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:07,140
It's romantic, right?

168
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:10,680
So it can be between two different people.

169
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:11,800
They meet each other one night.

170
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:17,000
They have a relationship for one night only, and then they never see each other again.

171
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:18,280
So it's casual.

172
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:18,500
It's casual.

173
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:20,240
Very good.

174
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:21,700
So yeah, you would see this.

175
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,280
You see this a lot in shows like, you know, Sex in the City and all that stuff.

176
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:27,740
Yeah, they have a lot of one night stands in that show.

177
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:28,420
All right.

178
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:30,080
So a one night stand.

179
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:36,700
And well, related to this, the girl was criticizing his friends, and she said, they're no saints.

180
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:37,740
Okay.

181
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:39,300
So we can say this in many different ways.

182
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:40,340
He's no saint.

183
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:41,520
She's no saint.

184
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:42,900
Or I'm no saint.

185
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:43,520
You're no saint.

186
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,000
So a saint is a good person.

187
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:52,380
It's a word that we use in religion to say someone who's like Mother Teresa, or someone who's just really good and kind.

188
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:53,660
Doesn't commit any sins.

189
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:54,320
Exactly.

190
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:58,620
And so to say, I'm no saint is to say, well, I do bad things, or I say bad things.

191
00:07:58,760 --> 00:07:59,760
I'm not perfect.

192
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:00,760
Okay.

193
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:02,360
So he or they, they're no saints.

194
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:03,760
They do bad stuff too.

195
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:04,600
Yeah, very good.

196
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,880
So that's the way that you would use, he's no saint, they're no saints.

197
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:09,000
All right.

198
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:13,280
So I think now we should listen to our dialogue for the last time, and we'll be right back.

199
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:24,760
Wow, that terrible movie is finally over.

200
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:27,790
Next time I'm picking the film because I don't want to end up seeing a chick flick.

201
00:08:28,650 --> 00:08:29,650
Well, you should have picked.

202
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,650
In the end, you always complain about everything.

203
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:34,190
Not everything, just this film.

204
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:37,080
Even the title was ridiculous.

205
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:38,050
And it's so long.

206
00:08:38,690 --> 00:08:41,630
Those were the two and a half most wasted hours of my life.

207
00:08:42,270 --> 00:08:45,570
So much so that I'm thinking about asking them to give me my money back.

208
00:08:46,270 --> 00:08:48,190
I'm thinking of taking you back home.

209
00:08:48,690 --> 00:08:51,390
I thought we could have a nice evening, but you're always so negative.

210
00:08:52,030 --> 00:08:57,510
I'm only complaining about a movie that I could have rented or bought and then thrown in the garbage.

211
00:08:58,150 --> 00:08:59,910
You see, that's what I'm talking about.

212
00:09:00,210 --> 00:09:02,590
I can't stand your sarcastic jokes anymore.

213
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:06,090
Next time, go with your gay friend who's more in touch with his feelings.

214
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:08,130
Well, he's more of a man than you are.

215
00:09:08,570 --> 00:09:10,190
At least he appreciates love stories.

216
00:09:10,890 --> 00:09:11,490
Love stories?

217
00:09:12,170 --> 00:09:13,570
More like one night stands.

218
00:09:14,070 --> 00:09:18,170
Don't criticize Mario, or else I'll start on those fat drunk friends of yours.

219
00:09:18,590 --> 00:09:19,130
They're no saints.

220
00:09:19,670 --> 00:09:20,110
My friends?

221
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:21,760
Fat?

222
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:23,350
What about those whales you call friends?

223
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:24,310
You're unbearable.

224
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:25,810
You can walk home.

225
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:26,270
I'm leaving.

226
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:28,160
Bye.

227
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:29,160
Good luck.

228
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:30,160
Bye.

229
00:09:40,630 --> 00:09:45,410
All right, so, Catherine, is this an argument that you would say is typical among couples?

230
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:47,790
Or maybe people that are probably about to break up?

231
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:51,010
Not couples that are happy, I would say.

232
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,690
But yeah, you definitely have people who are...

233
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:56,690
Well, I guess it's kind of the battle of the sexes, right?

234
00:09:56,840 --> 00:10:01,420
You have people who are different genders and so they don't always understand each other.

235
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:08,360
You know, you want to have your girlfriends as a girl and your girlfriends behave and talk in a certain way together and guys do the same thing but with their friends.

236
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:16,120
And so sometimes you have these clashes, these interactions that are negative and you know when people are upset with each other, they say mean things.

237
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:19,310
And especially I think in this case, they don't really like each other's friends.

238
00:10:19,770 --> 00:10:20,250
Exactly.

239
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:22,440
And that's a kind of a common thing.

240
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:29,600
Like a guy doesn't like his girlfriend's friends because maybe they gossip too much or something like that.

241
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:30,600
And the same thing with the girls, right?

242
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:32,570
Because a guy's friends maybe drink too much.

243
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:34,760
Drink too much, play too many card games.

244
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:35,760
They're single.

245
00:10:36,180 --> 00:10:42,740
So I think a lot of times these problems also have to do with the fact that like when a girl is with her girlfriend, she maybe acts differently.

246
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:44,600
Or when a guy is with his guy friends.

247
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:51,040
And so that's weird sometimes for the relationship because you see your partner acting differently with other people.

248
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:52,040
Right.

249
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:53,520
So, do you like your boyfriend or girlfriend's friends?

250
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,400
Do you get in fights about this kind of stuff?

251
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:56,400
Or you know what?

252
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:57,400
The in-laws.

253
00:10:57,400 --> 00:10:59,680
Oh, that's a whole another situation.

254
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:01,680
That's a whole another situation.

255
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:02,680
